a. The line $y=\frac32$ crosses the curve twice, so there are two solutions.

$\begin{aligned}3\sin(2x)&=\frac32\\\sin(2x)&=\frac12\end{aligned}$

Let $u=2x$, so $0\le u\le2\pi$.

$\begin{aligned}u&=\frac{\pi}{6},\frac{5\pi}{6}\\x&=\frac{u}{2}\\x&=\frac{\pi}{12},\frac{5\pi}{12}\end{aligned}$

b. The line $y=3$ touches the maximum once, at $x=\frac{\pi}{4}$. There is one solution.

c. The curve has range $[-3,3]$. The line $y=4$ misses it, so there are no solutions.

a. The cosine curve has period $\pi$. The horizontal line crosses it twice in each of the two cycles.

$\begin{aligned}3\cos(2x)+1&=\frac52\\3\cos(2x)&=\frac32\\\cos(2x)&=\frac12\end{aligned}$

Let $u=2x$, so $0\le u\le4\pi$.

$\begin{aligned}u&=\frac{\pi}{3},\frac{5\pi}{3},\frac{7\pi}{3},\frac{11\pi}{3}\\x&=\frac{u}{2}\\x&=\frac{\pi}{6},\frac{5\pi}{6},\frac{7\pi}{6},\frac{11\pi}{6}\end{aligned}$

