Each arm crosses the cosine curve twice, giving four solutions. Outside $[\pi-5,\pi+5]$, the V-shaped graph is above $1$, so there are no further intersections.

Rewrite the equation as $1+\sin\left(\frac{\pi x}{3}\right)=mx$. Its solutions are the intersections of the curve with $y=mx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{wave}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{boundary}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{steep}{HTML}{4F9B63}
\begin{axis}[width=10cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-0.35,xmax=6.5,ymin=-0.3,ymax=2.8,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},xtick={1.5,3,4.5,6},xticklabels={$\frac32$,$3$,$\frac92$,$6$},ytick={1,2},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},samples=180,clip=true]
\addplot[wave,thick,domain=0:6] {1+sin(deg(pi*x/3))};
\addplot[boundary,dashed,domain=0:6] {x/6};
\addplot[steep,dashed,domain=0:6] {x/3};
\addplot[only marks,black,mark=*] coordinates {(0,1) (4.5,0) (6,1)};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,anchor=south east] at (axis cs:6,1) {$(6,1)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Every line $y=mx$ passes through $(0,0)$.

For $m<0$, the line is below the non-negative curve for $x>0$, so there are no intersections.

For $m=0$, the line touches the curve only at its minimum $(\frac92,0)$, giving one solution.

For $m>0$, there is exactly one crossing before the minimum: the curve starts above the line, bends downwards on $[0,3]$, then decreases on $[3,\frac92]$ while the line rises.

After the minimum, the curve bends upwards towards $(6,1)$. A second intersection occurs when the line's endpoint height is at most $1$.

$\begin{aligned}6m&\le1\\m&\le\frac16\end{aligned}$

Thus $0<m\le\frac16$ gives two solutions, including the endpoint intersection when $m=\frac16$. For $m>\frac16$, there is only the first crossing.

Exactly one solution occurs when $m=0$ or $m>\frac16$.
